%%report item-completeness title: How complete items are entity: Item chart: bar x: field y: missing help: Stocked material, consumable, spare or service item. For each optional field, how many items leave it empty; the long bars are the fields nobody fills in. sql: SELECT 'inventory account' AS field, COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE inventory_account_id IS NULL) AS missing FROM bus_item WHERE deleted_at IS NULL UNION ALL SELECT 'expense account', COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE expense_account_id IS NULL) FROM bus_item WHERE deleted_at IS NULL UNION ALL SELECT 'valuation method', COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE valuation_method IS NULL) FROM bus_item WHERE deleted_at IS NULL ORDER BY 2 DESC
%%report warehouse-by-status title: Warehouses by status entity: Warehouse chart: pie x: status y: records help: Central, project or mobile inventory location. How warehouses divide across status, so the dominant group and the empty ones are both visible. sql: SELECT COALESCE(status::text, '(unset)') AS status, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_warehouse WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
%%report warehouse-by-warehouse-type title: Warehouses by warehouse type entity: Warehouse chart: pie x: warehouse_type y: records help: Central, project or mobile inventory location. How warehouses divide across warehouse type, so the dominant group and the empty ones are both visible. sql: SELECT COALESCE(warehouse_type::text, '(unset)') AS warehouse_type, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_warehouse WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
%%report warehouse-lifecycle title: Warehouse lifecycle entity: Warehouse chart: bar x: state y: records help: Central, project or mobile inventory location. Where warehouses sit in their state machine, in the order the diagram draws it; a state nothing has reached shows as zero. sql: SELECT d.state, COALESCE(c.records, 0) AS records FROM (SELECT 'draft' AS state, 0 AS position UNION ALL SELECT 'active', 1 UNION ALL SELECT 'cancelled', 2 UNION ALL SELECT 'closed', 3) d LEFT JOIN (SELECT status AS state, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_warehouse WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1) c ON c.state = d.state ORDER BY d.position
%%report warehouse-created-per-month title: Warehouses created per month entity: Warehouse chart: line x: month y: records help: Central, project or mobile inventory location. New warehouses per month over two years - the volume the business actually handles. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('month', created_at), 'YYYY-MM') AS month, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_warehouse WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND created_at >= now() - interval '24 months' GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
%%report warehouse-created-by-weekday title: Warehouses created by day of the week entity: Warehouse chart: bar x: weekday y: records help: Central, project or mobile inventory location. Which days warehouses are recorded on, which is the working rhythm of the people who enter them. sql: SELECT to_char(created_at, 'Dy') AS weekday, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_warehouse WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1, EXTRACT(isodow FROM created_at) ORDER BY EXTRACT(isodow FROM created_at)
%%report warehouse-changed-per-month title: Warehouses last changed per month entity: Warehouse chart: line x: month y: records help: Central, project or mobile inventory location. How recently warehouses were touched - a falling line is a register nobody is keeping up. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('month', updated_at), 'YYYY-MM') AS month, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_warehouse WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND updated_at >= now() - interval '24 months' GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
%%report warehouse-age-bands title: How recently warehouses were changed entity: Warehouse chart: bar x: last_changed y: records help: Central, project or mobile inventory location. Records by time since their last change; the oldest band is the stale tail to review or retire. sql: SELECT CASE WHEN updated_at >= now() - interval '30 days' THEN 'last 30 days' WHEN updated_at >= now() - interval '90 days' THEN '30 to 90 days' WHEN updated_at >= now() - interval '180 days' THEN '90 to 180 days' ELSE 'older than 180 days' END AS last_changed, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_warehouse WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY MIN(updated_at) DESC
%%report warehouse-edit-churn title: How often warehouses are edited entity: Warehouse chart: bar x: edits y: records help: Central, project or mobile inventory location. Records by how many times they have been saved; the heavily edited ones are where the process is being fought. sql: SELECT CASE WHEN version <= 1 THEN 'never edited' WHEN version <= 3 THEN 'edited once or twice' WHEN version <= 9 THEN 'edited 3 to 8 times' ELSE 'edited 9 or more times' END AS edits, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_warehouse WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY MIN(version)
%%report warehouse-completeness title: How complete warehouses are entity: Warehouse chart: bar x: field y: missing help: Central, project or mobile inventory location. For each optional field, how many warehouses leave it empty; the long bars are the fields nobody fills in. sql: SELECT 'project' AS field, COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE project_id IS NULL) AS missing FROM bus_warehouse WHERE deleted_at IS NULL UNION ALL SELECT 'address', COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE address IS NULL) FROM bus_warehouse WHERE deleted_at IS NULL UNION ALL SELECT 'manager', COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE manager_id IS NULL) FROM bus_warehouse WHERE deleted_at IS NULL ORDER BY 2 DESC
%%report inventory-transaction-by-transaction-type title: Inventory Transactions by transaction type entity: InventoryTransaction chart: pie x: transaction_type y: records help: Receipt, issue, transfer, return or adjustment of inventory. How inventory transactions divide across transaction type, so the dominant group and the empty ones are both visible. sql: SELECT COALESCE(transaction_type::text, '(unset)') AS transaction_type, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_inventory_transaction WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
%%report inventory-transaction-created-per-month title: Inventory Transactions created per month entity: InventoryTransaction chart: line x: month y: records help: Receipt, issue, transfer, return or adjustment of inventory. New inventory transactions per month over two years - the volume the business actually handles. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('month', created_at), 'YYYY-MM') AS month, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_inventory_transaction WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND created_at >= now() - interval '24 months' GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
